"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,361\nThe Learning Brain. Series 2: Memory\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,340\nEpisode 4\n\n3\n00:00:07,420 --> 00:00:09,397\nMake it, don\u2019t take it\n\n4\n00:00:12,197 --> 00:00:14,030\nTwo ways to meet the same word.\n\n5\n00:00:14,441 --> 00:00:16,368\nRead it: rapid, fast.\n\n6\n00:00:16,501 --> 00:00:17,221\nOr make it: rapid,\n\n7\n00:00:17,477 --> 00:00:18,734\nf blank.\n\n8\n00:00:19,262 --> 00:00:21,160\nThe second takes a moment of effort.\n\n9\n00:00:21,510 --> 00:00:22,757\nIn study after study,\n\n10\n00:00:22,777 --> 00:00:27,303\nwords people generated were remembered better than words they simply read.\n\n11\n00:00:27,473 --> 00:00:29,360\nThis is the generation effect.\n\n12\n00:00:29,719 --> 00:00:31,358\nAcross eighty six studies,\n\n13\n00:00:31,445 --> 00:00:35,277\ngenerating beat reading by almost half a standard deviation.\n\n14\n00:00:35,747 --> 00:00:37,193\nIt held for synonyms,\n\n15\n00:00:37,213 --> 00:00:39,531\nopposites, categories, and rhymes,\n\n16\n00:00:39,741 --> 00:00:41,898\nand for both recognition and recall.\n\n17\n00:00:42,357 --> 00:00:44,920\nProducing an answer, even an easy one,\n\n18\n00:00:45,110 --> 00:00:47,544\nleaves a stronger trace than receiving it.\n\n19\n00:00:47,714 --> 00:00:50,290\nThe same principle scales up to understanding.\n\n20\n00:00:50,785 --> 00:00:54,577\nSelf explanation means pausing to explain why a step works,\n\n21\n00:00:54,863 --> 00:00:57,318\nor how an idea connects to another.\n\n22\n00:00:57,974 --> 00:01:00,628\nA meta-analysis of sixty four studies,\n\n23\n00:01:00,724 --> 00:01:02,824\nwith nearly six thousand learners,\n\n24\n00:01:03,134 --> 00:01:09,275\nfound that prompting students to self explain improved learning by about half a standard deviation,\n\n25\n00:01:09,544 --> 00:01:11,042\nacross many subjects.\n\n26\n00:01:11,212 --> 00:01:13,738\nGeneration even helps before learning.\n\n27\n00:01:14,131 --> 00:01:17,529\nStudents answered questions about an essay before reading it.\n\n28\n00:01:17,944 --> 00:01:19,630\nMost of their guesses were wrong.\n\n29\n00:01:20,048 --> 00:01:24,828\nYet those items were learned better than ones given the same extra time to study.\n\n30\n00:01:25,383 --> 00:01:28,560\nA failed attempt prepares the mind for the answer.\n\n31\n00:01:28,730 --> 00:01:29,755\nOne caution.\n\n32\n00:01:30,122 --> 00:01:33,261\nIn these studies, the correct answer always followed.\n\n33\n00:01:33,666 --> 00:01:37,352\nA wrong guess left uncorrected is just a wrong guess.\n\n34\n00:01:37,721 --> 00:01:41,051\nGeneration works when students can make something meaningful,\n\n35\n00:01:41,256 --> 00:01:43,471\nand get the right answer soon after.\n\n36\n00:01:43,641 --> 00:01:44,425\nFor teaching.\n\n37\n00:01:44,795 --> 00:01:49,655\nBefore a lecture, pose two or three questions students cannot yet answer,\n\n38\n00:01:50,070 --> 00:01:52,497\nthen teach.\n\n39\n00:01:52,854 --> 00:01:55,038\nLeave gaps in notes for students to fill.\n\n40\n00:01:55,307 --> 00:01:56,540\nPause after a worked step and ask: why does this follow?\n\n41\n00:01:56,785 --> 00:01:59,364\nHave students predict a result before you show it.\n\n42\n00:01:59,708 --> 00:02:02,440\nAnd always close the loop with the correct answer.\n\n43\n00:02:02,610 --> 00:02:04,339\nWhat students make, they keep.\n\n44\n00:02:04,547 --> 00:02:05,993\nLet them make it first.\n\n"
